Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:46,960
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guest today is Reed Duramas, Growth Product Manager at Substack.

7
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:53,120
Prior to joining Substack, Reed helped grow subscriptions at Hulu, Crunchyroll, and HBO Max.

8
00:00:53,600 --> 00:01:05,120
On the podcast, I talk with Reed about whether or not to increase your price, how to execute if you do, and why price increases often impact growth more than retention.

9
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,640
Hey Reed, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:09,840
Thanks for having me.

11
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:10,960
Really excited to be here.

12
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:12,880
So I wanted to have you on.

13
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:20,160
I've read a bunch of your posts this year, and whenever I read a great post, I think gosh more people need to read this.

14
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:24,400
So I now work with my colleagues publish a subclub newsletter.

15
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:27,120
So I've shared your posts, I think in three different newsletters.

16
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,200
And then I always think reading a great post, I wonder what else is behind this?

17
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:34,880
Like what would they say about this situation that wasn't covered?

18
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:36,720
I said, hey, I've got a podcast.

19
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,720
I figured I'd have you on and we could talk through some of your posts.

20
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:46,480
So we'll link to the posts and your growth croissant blog in the show notes.

21
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,400
So people can go follow along if they want.

22
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:49,600
Really great stuff.

23
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:51,280
We're not going to like read the posts.

24
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:55,600
We're not going to cover everything, but I want to kind of hit some of these major topics.

25
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,280
So the most recent one.

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,160
you wrote and I think is super applicable right now that a lot of apps are thinking, should I increase my price?

27
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,880
And then if I am going to increase my price, how do I do it and not get the backlash and everything?

28
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,680
So I wanted to kick off talking about that.

29
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,960
So let's just start with, should you raise your price?

30
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:23,040
Well, thanks again for having me on and really appreciate the kind words.

31
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:26,960
A lot of times, like, you'll write a newsletter post and you don't know if it lands or not.

32
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:29,520
So really appreciate you and appreciate you sharing it.

33
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:39,600
The price one was one that was timely because Spotify, which I've subscribed to for the past decade is doing their first price increase.

34
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,440
And I think they've kind of made it through by now.

35
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,680
And there's a lot of writing out there.

36
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:55,840
And it was something that we wrestled with a lot in the streaming world because it's like one of the most impactful ways to drive up customer lifetime value and drive up revenue.

37
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:01,040
But there's this really hard balancing act with executing a price increase well.

38
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:03,520
And the trade-offs are usually like.

39
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:11,520
how much near-term revenue gain can I try to capture and without sacrificing your long-term growth potential?

40
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,440
Because if you go too hard into the price increase, you may get a lot of.

41
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,880
revenue early, but it could kind of erode your growth velocity.

42
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:25,120
One specific metric to think about is your year over year growth in paid subscribers.

43
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:29,120
That's the one that you kind of want to pay attention to after you do these price increases.

44
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:36,640
And yeah, we can go a lot of different ways from there, but I'm actually curious to get your take on Disney Plus and how this has played out.

45
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:43,920
So they actually again announced a price increase recently, lambasted in the press, worst time ever to do a price increase.

46
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:54,480
Everybody's complaining about Disney Plus quality going downhill, but it's interesting because they actually started at five dollars and that kind of what you were saying and what you talk about in the.

47
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:02,400
post about growth versus profitability starting at five dollars is probably a big reason why they grew so big, so quick.

48
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,280
It was just a no brainer cheap decision for a lot of families and people to stream.

49
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:10,400
But now they're at that phase where, okay, growth is over.

50
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,240
Where's revenue growth going to come from?

51
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,320
More price raises.

52
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:22,880
So do you think Disney Plus got it right even with the backlash they're getting today in raising their prices now versus maybe having charged more early on?

53
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:25,440
And it wasn't just Disney Plus.

54
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:36,800
I think when we were at Crunchyroll, even Hulu too, the primary metric was paid subscribers and baked into that was this assumption that over time you could gradually increase price.

55
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:44,080
I think Disney launched at a really low price point and a lot of people were getting Disney Plus for free as part of Verizon.

56
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,560
They had like a pretty big partnership with them.

57
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,240
They had really discounted annual plans at launch, if I remember correctly.

58
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:55,840
And of course, they bundled it with ESPN Plus and Hulu, which I think a lot of people bought that as well.

59
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:02,080
So when Disney Plus launched, there was a lot of, oh my gosh, they got to like 100 million subscribers so quickly.

60
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:11,920
But you got to like dig into the numbers there and look at revenue per subscriber and really try to figure out, are they actually catching up with Netflix or are they still pretty far behind?

61
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,880
And I think if you looked under the covers, the revenue per subscription wasn't quite there.

62
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:19,760
Whether they're doing the right thing now, it's what they have to do.

63
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:26,000
Like if you look at the streaming space broadly right now, it's not the easiest space to be operating in.

64
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:37,840
It's been fascinating over the past decade watching a lot of these traditional media companies try to move from cable TV into streaming video where the skill sets needed to grow those products.

65
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:39,040
are very different.

66
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:49,680
You have to think about direct to consumer relationships and how to grow subscribers that way versus doing carrier deals with a handful or more pay TV operators.

67
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,920
Totally different skill set and the economics are really different.

68
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:58,720
So I think it's been kind of a wild west and there's a lot of volatility right now.

69
00:05:58,880 --> 00:05:59,840
There's a lot of MA.

70
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,320
I think that consolidation will continue.

71
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,520
Whether they handled it right or not, I don't really know.

72
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,120
And I don't know if it really matters.

73
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:09,280
I think they're doing what they kind of have to do at this point.

74
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:11,680
A lot of other streamers too.

75
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:18,800
In that is kind of, and you talk about this in your posts, is that you will get some level of pushback.

76
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:27,680
And I mean, whenever somebody has to pay more for what they perceive to already be getting, there is going to be pushback.

77
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:32,320
I'm a father of four kids and inflation is hitting pretty hard.

78
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:38,240
I mean, I'm getting a little more sympathetic to maybe always raising prices isn't the best idea.

79
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:45,840
How do you think about the consumer sentiment part of the decision of whether to raise prices or not?

80
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:54,880
Well, one thing you mentioned there that I think is important is if you're asking them to pay more for what they perceive as the same product, it's a tough sell.

81
00:06:55,280 --> 00:07:02,000
Maybe a marginal price increase will go through okay, like a one dollar price increase, two dollar price increase.

82
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:06,080
That seems to be kind of the standard set by Spotify and Netflix.

83
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:11,120
Each time they raise price, they're not doubling their price in one swing, they're kind of marginally going up.

84
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:20,000
The thing that we've seen consumer products do well when they're doing a price increase is reaffirm the value prop that people are paying for.

85
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:28,160
And you can also tease any upcoming changes that would like materially improve the product, the value prop.

86
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,920
And I think that can really help get you through these price increases.

87
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,400
And so I think communications are really, really important.

88
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:50,560
And one of the things you mentioned in the post too was actually not just teasing future features, but maybe trying to coordinate the launch of a big new feature with the price increase., like how would you pull that off?

89
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:51,440
Exactly.

90
00:07:51,680 --> 00:08:02,880
Like if you were Disney and you had some huge Star Wars series in the pipeline, I would try to time the price increase roughly around when those shows are going to premiere.

91
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:08,080
And you could say in the comments, this is what we're doing with, we're not just asking you for more money for the sake of it.

92
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:12,960
And don't blame a price increase on like inflation or something that consumers can't control.

93
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:16,960
That's kind of like one of those uncontrollable factors, like cost of gas is going up.

94
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:18,720
Sorry, we got to increase our price.

95
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:29,200
It's usually best to focus on your product and how it's getting better and how you're going to use this extra money to continue to make the product better.

96
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:34,400
And keep in mind, the people who are paying for it already definitely value your product in some way.

97
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:40,240
So they're going to be usually thrilled with that idea of you trying to make a concerted effort to continue to improve.

98
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:49,440
The thing that Disney, I think, actually did really well, maybe is that they timed the press announcement before two big series.

99
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:58,080
So Ahsoka was just released this week as we're recording, and then they have a big Marvel show coming out, I think in the next three or four weeks.

100
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:00,960
So they announced it two or three weeks ahead of that.

101
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:05,440
They don't go into effect until mid series with these big new shows.

102
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,800
And so they kind of did what you're suggesting and they took the PR lumps ahead.

103
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:14,640
And now people are excited again because these big shows are coming.

104
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:25,520
And by the time the price increase actually takes effect, they're going to be mid show on these exciting new shows that I think Disney had a lot of confidence that were going to be very attractive to their subscribers.

105
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:26,800
That's exactly right.

106
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:28,640
I mean, that's the best way to do it.

107
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:37,120
Like if you're Spotify, you don't want to lose a major podcast or Led Zeppelin or the Beatles catalogue right before you do a price increase.

108
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:38,800
The timing piece is really important.

109
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:40,800
The communication is really important.

110
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:47,200
So we've been talking about huge players, Disney, Spotify, a dollar increase in Spotify.

111
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,240
At this point, most people listen to music.

112
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:52,960
The way they listen to music is streaming.

113
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:57,360
So there's not really that much question of like, am I going to cancel Spotify?

114
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:01,040
It's maybe more, am I going to switch from Spotify to Apple Music?

115
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:03,840
There's only really two or three big players out there.

116
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:05,280
I mean, what do you think?

117
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:11,600
And have you done much thought on these smaller subscription apps where they don't have the brand name?

118
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,080
They don't necessarily have the kind of default.

119
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:15,680
People are going to stay around.

120
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,280
Are there any tips on how a smaller app should think about a price increase?

121
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:23,600
Let me bring it into like the Substack world.

122
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:27,360
world because these are effectively all their own subscription media business.

123
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:28,720
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

124
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:41,600
And one thing that we've talked with writers about, sometimes we'll do these reviews, you know, and we'll go in and we'll look at their retention metrics, their growth rate, all these metrics to evaluate the health of their business.

125
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:46,800
And sometimes we'll say, hey, your paid retention rates are like through the roof.

126
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:47,840
They're incredible.

127
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,200
They're way better than your peers.

128
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:56,000
And what we'll usually see is they're like, hell yeah, and then just totally tune out and feel reassured.

129
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:04,880
But there's a flip side to that, which is that can also mean that your product is underpriced or that you're not being aggressive enough trying to grow your audience.

130
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:09,520
And so the phrase that we try to hammer home is your health metrics are too good.

131
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:14,160
You know, it's like going into the doctor's office and they're like, look, you need to like eat a cheeseburger or something.

132
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:16,560
I think that's actually one thing.

133
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:23,120
I see a lot of writers on Substack that I think are underpriced and they're not realizing their full earnings potential.

134
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:26,000
In part because they set their price a long time ago.

135
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,240
It's a hard, intimidating decision.

136
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:32,000
And after you make that decision, you don't really want to think about it again.

137
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:37,360
So you just see a lot of prices kind of not changed even over the course of multiple years.

138
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:41,600
And I feel like they're not realizing their full earnings potential.

139
00:11:42,160 --> 00:12:01,200
That's a great way to think about it because if in our consumer subscription app world that we mostly talk about here on the podcast, if you're seeing way above median retention rates, if you're seeing way above median conversion to subscription rates, that might actually be the sign that you're leaving money on the table.

140
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:16,160
But then on the flip side, if you're seeing twenty percent retention, which at RevenueCat, we shared a state of subscription apps report where median retention for a consumer subscription app on an annual plan is somewhere in the thirty percent range.

141
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:22,480
So there's a lot of apps that have way lower than that, but then there's also a lot of apps that have way higher retention.

142
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:31,840
But if you're in that 20% retention on your annual subscriptions, probably signal that you're not in a good place to raise prices on your existing subscribers.

143
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:40,400
I didn't really think about kind of looking at those sorts of metrics as part of the decision making process and whether to raise a price or not.

144
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:48,160
We see some writers on Substack retaining 90% of their subscribers after one year, which to me is just like totally bonkers.

145
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:48,720
Yeah.

146
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:56,160
And I want to give a shout out to Antenna, which is like a data product that really looks at retention for the streamers.

147
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:58,960
And they have some really great retention data.

148
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:04,960
If you are operating like a bigger consumer subscription product, that can be a good source of information to compare and contrast.

149
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:06,560
It's obviously awesome.

150
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:10,400
If your attention is above your peers or above normal, that's great news.

151
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:14,880
But I too often see people put their feet on the table and be like, nice, let's let it cruise.

152
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:20,240
I think it's good to think about, well, maybe we're leaving some money on the table.

153
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:21,680
Maybe we're a little underpriced.

154
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:32,080
So then that's actually the next thing I wanted to get into is like, how do you model out whether you are leaving money on the table or not?

155
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,720
And then how do you think about the potential for churn when you do raise your price?

156
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:53,680
I mean, I can't show Excel spreadsadsheets in a podcast, but I mean, talk me through kind of the high level thinking of how to drop that into a data warehouse or a spreadsheet or how do you model this out to see if it's really worth it in the long run?

157
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:55,920
Are you sacrificing growth in the short term?

158
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:57,840
Are you sacrificing retention in the long run?

159
00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:59,440
I mean, there's a lot of factors here.

160
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:05,520
The easiest avenues to break it out at the highest level is to think about your existing subscribers.

161
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,800
Let's assume you're adjusting the price for your existing subscribers.

162
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:13,120
A lot of people when they do a price increase, they only do it for new subscribers in the future.

163
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:24,560
And that's a fine approach, but just know that you're leaving the vast majority of the impact from a price increase on the table to really realize the revenue gained from a price increase.

164
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:28,160
It's important to figure out a way to increase price for existing subscribers.

165
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:38,960
I would caveat that to say, if you're really early in your journey and there's not that many subscribers, that's the best time to just grandfather people in and not piss people off, not mess with your churn.

166
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:43,680
Like, later stages when that's maybe most applicable, but that's part of the calculation, right?

167
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:45,840
Like, how many existing subscribers do I have?

168
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,960
And if I raise price on all of them, is it actually meaningful revenue and worth doing?

169
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:52,640
That's a great point.

170
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:59,840
Keep in mind to the people you're talking about in that early stage are people who are your founding members.

171
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:03,840
I remember the first person who paid for my newsletter, I'm like, you're God's gift to Earth.

172
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,360
You know, like, thank you so much.

173
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:09,200
I would happily never increase their price.

174
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:13,840
That's an important dimension to think about your early supporters and to make sure you're taking care of them.

175
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:23,840
But yeah, I think at a certain point, if you're at like a Netflix scale or a Spotify scale, even Strava, Duolingo, you have probably enough paid subscribers at that point.

176
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:30,320
to think about most of the value of a price increase coming from your existing subscribers.

177
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:39,600
If you assume we're kind of in that world, it's good to break out the impact by the revenue gain from existing subscribers versus future flow of new subscribers coming in.

178
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:49,600
What we typically see when we've done price increases in the past is that they usually impact subscriber acquisition way more than subscriber retention.

179
00:15:50,080 --> 00:15:55,600
Now that's assuming you kind of execute the price increase well and that you're not going bonkers with it.

180
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:56,720
You're not doubling your price.

181
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,640
You know, you're doing the more incremental approach.

182
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:10,400
But usually if you execute well, you won't see much churn from your existing subscriber base, but you will see a little pressure on new subscriber acquisition.

183
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:17,680
So say you're adding 100,000 new subscribers per month and you increase the price by ten percent.

184
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:28,080
It's not crazy to see like a little bit of a step function drop down, you know, maybe ninety thousand a month or eighty thousand a month, just kind of pulling money numbers out of thin air here.

185
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:36,560
But the point being, the expectation should be that you're going to see a little bit of a slowdown on acquisition, whereas retention, you might not see too much of an impact.

186
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:43,520
And again, that's why it's important to like figure out a way to thoughtfully increase price on existing subscribers.

187
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:53,520
The basic equation is like, do you want fewer subscribers paying you more money or do you want more subscribers paying you less money?

188
00:16:54,320 --> 00:17:00,800
And then there's really a very few who can achieve the kind of more subscribers paying you more money.

189
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:02,080
It does happen.

190
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:14,400
One of the things a Spotify and a Disney Plus and others can't do because they are such a big brand is actually test the price increase on new users before they actually raise a price on existing users.

191
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:29,360
And maybe that's actually a great way to think about it for apps that can get away with that is if you're not a brand name, if people don't know your price offhand, if the press isn't going to write about your price, then you can actually go ahead and see first what the impact is on new subscribers.

192
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:32,480
And then if you find that sweet spot, maybe that's the time to.

193
00:17:33,040 --> 00:17:37,360
then start thinking about rolling that price out to all the existing subscribers.

194
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:39,680
Yeah, there's a lot of different ways to go about it.

195
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:42,160
Testing price increases is really hard.

196
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:51,360
If one person sees two different prices and they kind of figure out what you're doing, that can make its way to X or Reddit, and then you've got a thing to deal with.

197
00:17:51,680 --> 00:17:54,480
But yeah, I mean, any kind of signal there can be helpful.

198
00:17:54,800 --> 00:18:00,720
Surveys can sometimes be somewhat helpful, but people say different things than what they end up doing.

199
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:04,400
So I wouldn't put all your trust in those results.

200
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:08,400
In those cases where you have raised prices in the past, have you done surveys?

201
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:15,840
Is there kind of price elasticity surveying that you've done and was it helpful or do you feel like you really just have to experiment?

202
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:27,520
We've definitely done a lot of surveys and not only to find like what our core price should be but to think about what our most passionate fans would pay relative to like your core subscriber.

203
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:31,440
It's kind of like you want to separate out what the top one percent are willing to pay.

204
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:43,520
They maybe have more price elasticity or a higher willingness to pay a higher price for maybe not even that much different of a product relative to like your core product, which is what most people are buying.

205
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:47,040
The equivalent on Substack would be we have a founding member tier.

206
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,720
And if people choose that option, they can actually fill in their own price.

207
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:56,080
And we see a lot of people go way beyond what the retail price is on the founding member.

208
00:18:56,480 --> 00:18:58,960
And some of those people just really want to support the writer.

209
00:18:59,200 --> 00:18:59,920
That's great to see.

210
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:14,160
Yeah, I was actually going to bring that up, that one other option, and we even see this in streaming and Spotify and others, is that one way to raise your price without raising your price is to add a higher tier.

211
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:19,680
So we were talking earlier about adding a big new feature at the same time you're raising your price.

212
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:27,360
Well, another way to potentially do that is add that big new feature as a new premium tier on top of the existing tiers.

213
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:31,920
I wonder if that's actually a good optiontion than raising your price for some apps.

214
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:33,920
It's actually starting to do the tiering thing.

215
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:34,960
Do you have any thoughts there?

216
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:37,680
And the, I mean, the problem with tiering is complexity.

217
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,600
Is it how many prices are you showing?

218
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:41,200
Your paywall gets confusing.

219
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:44,800
What are your thoughts on tiering as a way to raise prices?

220
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:46,800
I think tiers make a ton of sense.

221
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:50,240
You know, without them, you're kind of forcing one price on everyone.

222
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:55,200
When you know that people have different willingness to pay, I think it's good to keep it as simple as possible.

223
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:58,480
More than three tiers, the consumer's brain might start to break.

224
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:06,000
The other thing that we're seeing in streaming is the introduction of ad based subscription tiers at the lower end.

225
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:20,160
And I think a lot of this is just reaffirming your point around you're trying to think about how many paid subscribers you can realistically get and what the revenue per subscriber is and how they break that out across different tiers, subscription tiers.

226
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:31,280
And so if you're somebody like Netflix in the US and you're really bumping up against what could be your total addressable market, then introducing a lower price ad tier.

227
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:32,480
could make sense.

228
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:34,320
Same thing with the other streamers.

229
00:20:34,880 --> 00:20:50,400
What we see in more of a passion driven world, like the substacks of the world or the Patreons of the world, there's this element of like the top 1% of fans who have like a much stronger set of feelings about their subscription to a certain product.

230
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:52,000
We saw this at Crunchyroll.

231
00:20:52,320 --> 00:21:01,200
We had this thing called the t-shirt test, which was like, if you wear the t-shirt of that brand, then it's saying something about who you are, about your identity.

232
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:07,280
And when you see somebody else wearing that Crunchyroll t-shirt, you have this immediate connection, this immediate trust.

233
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:12,560
And it's a great symbol for the depth of relationship somebody feels about a brand.

234
00:21:13,200 --> 00:21:15,760
And so, yeah, not all subscriptions are created equal.

235
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:27,680
And so I think something like Crunchyroll probably has a better shot at like a higher priced subscription tier that has some other stuff in it, whereas Netflix is highest priced here is more like a family plan.

236
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:29,360
It's a functional value prop.

237
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:45,280
I think that point you just brought up about Netflix hitting their potential total addressable market in the US is actually something a lot of apps are maybe hitting and not quite realizing what wall they're hitting.

238
00:21:45,920 --> 00:21:59,680
I've heard a lot of folks in the industry say for everything from fitness apps to other apps in the space that you hit this ten to twenty million a year ARR and very few apps can break beyond that.

239
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:02,080
And maybe that's that, you know, you're not Netflix.

240
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:05,200
You're not going to have 100 million subscribers or whatever it is.

241
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:09,440
So these apps may be hitting that total serviceable market.

242
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:16,640
Like, yes, everyone works out, but realistically your fitness app is not going to be subscribed.

243
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:18,400
There's so much competition in that space.

244
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:20,000
People have so many different needs.

245
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:25,120
Once you hit that ten, twenty million dollars a year, you might actually be bumping up against it.

246
00:22:25,360 --> 00:22:29,600
And so then that's the time to then start thinking, okay, what are these tiers?

247
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:33,120
How do we start letting people who are willing to pay more pay more?

248
00:22:33,680 --> 00:22:41,520
Visco is an app I use all the time and they raise prices and I still think it's cheap because it's like that's just where I go when I have a photo I really like.

249
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:43,280
I've been using the FitBod app.

250
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:47,360
And it's just been so perfect for my at home workouts.

251
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:49,040
And it's 60 bucks a year.

252
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:51,840
I've paid trainers 60 bucks an hour to come train me.

253
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:57,520
I would pay so much more because how well that product is meeting my needs.

254
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:08,240
So yeah, I wonder if that total serviceable market is what some apps are starting to bump up against and need to start looking at how these bigger players are starting to diversify income streams.

255
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:10,080
I think that's exactly right.

256
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,080
It's hard to know when you're bumping up against that ceiling.

257
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:22,480
The question we always got hit with at Crunchyroll was like, how many people will actually pay for an anime only streaming service?

258
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:25,680
When we got there, we had 200,000 subs.

259
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:30,720
And I remember us being so skeptical of ever getting beyond where we were.

260
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:34,240
The idea of getting to a million, mind blowingly hard.

261
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:40,240
There was weeks where we would lose like a thousand subs, you know, or really slow, inching along pacing.

262
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:44,000
And we just kept getting that question, like, how many people will actually pay for this?

263
00:23:44,560 --> 00:23:47,840
Fast forward to today, and I think there are over 12 million paid subscribers.

264
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:50,560
And we didn't increase price for a long time.

265
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:56,240
We really wanted to exhaust all the possible ways of bringing on new subscribers.

266
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:03,360
Not to mention, like, we tried to launch other products that were like solving tangential needs for people who were canceling Crunchyroll.

267
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:15,200
So we tried a bunch of different things and only got to a price increase pretty deep into my time there, you know, after six or seven years of really focusing on paid subscribers and scaling that up as much as possible.

268
00:24:15,840 --> 00:24:20,320
I'm honestly shocked that Crunchyroll did get to twelve million subscribers.

269
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:24,560
I think there are maybe like two things going on simultaneously here.

270
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:32,320
And I want to hear one about how you at Crunchyroll started to solve this and found new ways to grow and expanded that audience.

271
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:46,320
But I think there's a second thing at play here that it's kind of the rising tide lifts all boats that consumers are more and more more willing to pay via subscriptions for things they care about, for things they value.

272
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:48,080
And yes, there is subscription fatigue.

273
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:50,400
People complain about how many subscriptions they have.

274
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:55,840
But then look at the industry, it's growing because when you deliver value, people are willing to pay.

275
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:01,040
So I think, I wonder if some of Crunchyroll's growth was part of that rising tide.

276
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:03,920
But then I'm sure you all must have done a lot of work.

277
00:25:04,160 --> 00:25:12,800
So tell me about what it took to get from those tens of thousands of subscribers and not even believing you could hit a million to twelve million subscribers, which is just incredible.

278
00:25:13,360 --> 00:25:15,040
I mean, we could talk for a whole day.

279
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:16,480
Give me the highlights.

280
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:17,360
Give me the highlights.

281
00:25:17,520 --> 00:25:21,360
So the highest level, we thought a lot about how do we acquire more subscribers?

282
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:23,600
How do we retain our existing subscribers?

283
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:25,520
There's a lot of sub-bullets in both of those.

284
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:30,320
We did a lot of marketing stuff, a lot of partnership stuff, and those were really important.

285
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:35,280
But at the end of the day, what's the most important was like what shows we had on the service.

286
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:38,400
And then we tried to build a strong brand around that.

287
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:49,760
And back to the t-shirt thing, it wasn't always about building the best video player or the best payments engine or the best website or apps.

288
00:25:50,800 --> 00:25:56,160
We were able to compete on kind of a different dimension because of the deeper relationship we had with our audience.

289
00:25:56,400 --> 00:26:08,000
And so we thought a lot about how do we build out a business around our subscribers and introduce other revenue streams like merchandise events and gaming and a bunch of different things.

290
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:16,000
And so that was useful because we didn't have to like have the best video player in line with Netflix or Amazon Prime of the world.

291
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:19,120
But one of the things you mentioned there is expanding show.

292
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:34,080
So ultimately, and you talk about this in a lot of your blog posts, including the price increase, is that ultimately the amount of money you're going to be able to charge and the revenue you're going to get per customer just really boils down to how much value you're delivering.

293
00:26:34,320 --> 00:26:38,080
So it sounds like a lot of that growth had to do with just how do we deliver?

294
00:26:38,800 --> 00:26:40,240
more and more and more value?

295
00:26:40,880 --> 00:26:41,600
That's exactly right.

296
00:26:41,840 --> 00:26:45,920
And I know that's like opaque and flippant to say, but it really is all that matters.

297
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:48,000
And so what do you do from that?

298
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:54,560
You got to really figure out why people are buying your product and try to double down as much as possible on that.

299
00:26:55,520 --> 00:27:04,640
But the hard part is you also got to keep an eye on why some of your audience is canceling and whether you can start trying to solve some of their problems and gradually expand.

300
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:07,600
from your core of your subscriber base.

301
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:09,760
One example from Crunchyroll.

302
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:17,040
When we got there, most of the people who were paid subscribers to Crunchyroll at that time were the most hardcore anime fans in the world.

303
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:22,240
And we had to adjust how we welcomed new anime fans.

304
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:24,800
And that's like a branding and vibe thing.

305
00:27:25,120 --> 00:27:27,200
And that showed up in some of the marketing stuff we did.

306
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:29,040
But that was a big change.

307
00:27:29,440 --> 00:27:36,080
And honestly, it took a little bit of a change within the company from a cultural perspective because the company was all diehard anime fans.

308
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:44,000
And so yeah, I think there is mechanics like that to be mindful of and to think about like, okay, I need to figure out the value that my existing subscribers are getting.

309
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:45,760
I need to make sure I'm reinforcing that.

310
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:54,480
But I also need to be mindful of how to slowly expand from my core audience today too, if I want to continue driving subscriber growth.

311
00:27:54,640 --> 00:27:55,280
How do you figure that out?

312
00:27:55,360 --> 00:27:59,440
I mean, you actually talk about this in several other posts, but let's talk through aspects of that.

313
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:03,440
You survey your users, you product analytics.

314
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:06,960
How did you figure out who those core users were and what kept them?

315
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:16,480
And then how did you figure out who those lighter users, who they were, and then how to bring them deeper into the Crunchyroll experience?

316
00:28:17,120 --> 00:28:19,440
Surveys are crucial, and there's different ways to do that.

317
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:28,880
You could do like live interviews, pick up the phone, talk to some of your most passionate, longest tenured subscribers, figure out why they have been around for a while.

318
00:28:29,600 --> 00:28:34,160
You could talk to people who are canceling right away and try to get a sense of what's the difference here.

319
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:40,400
One easy analysis we did pretty consistently in the streaming world was this was back when free trials were a thing.

320
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:46,560
You could do it with like the first billing month as well, but look at the people who are canceling their subscription early on.

321
00:28:47,440 --> 00:28:59,120
Look at the people who are making it through that period and look for the behavior that is different about those two groups and try to figure out early on what is indicative of risky behavior.

322
00:28:59,680 --> 00:29:01,520
What is signaling valuable behavior?

323
00:29:01,920 --> 00:29:03,520
Try to bend the product in those directions.

324
00:29:03,840 --> 00:29:07,760
That onboarding period is like the most crucial part of a subscriber journey.

325
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:22,960
And that could be the first day, the first app session, or the first month that somebody's us usually the place to focus if you're really trying to figure out why people are getting value from what you're providing and trying to figure out how to bend the product more toward that direction.

326
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:26,720
That's a more objective data analysis exercise.

327
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:34,080
But yeah, I would definitely do the surveys and you could do that as part of onboarding or just kind of ad hoc to your whole audience.

328
00:29:34,320 --> 00:29:35,520
And there's different flavors of that.

329
00:29:35,680 --> 00:29:44,720
You could just send them like a Google form or you could literally hop on a zoom and talk to some people and really dig into what they value and what they would like to see from your product.

330
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:50,240
So for the people who are canceling, how do you really sus out?

331
00:29:50,560 --> 00:29:55,840
Like there's co, I think you say in one of your posts, there's three buckets.

332
00:29:55,920 --> 00:30:04,560
It's like the people who subscribe and say subscribe, the people who cancel but you actually have potential with them, and the other people who are just not worth chasing.

333
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:07,280
But how do you figure out who is who?

334
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:23,440
And then especially, I think a lot of people will be thinking, okay, the people who cancel but are actually winnable, how do you sus out what's actually going to be valuable enough to keep them subscribed and go after that market?

335
00:30:24,080 --> 00:30:26,160
Yeah, there's like gradients here, I guess.

336
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:33,360
There are some people who maybe try your product and they ask for something that's totally abstract, totally different than what you're trying to do.

337
00:30:33,520 --> 00:30:37,360
In a lot of those cases, I think you just got to say, okay, well, thanks for coming by.

338
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:38,320
We'll see you later.

339
00:30:38,720 --> 00:30:43,680
Then there are people who are asking for things that are not totally different than what you're doing today.

340
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:53,440
For example, with Crunchyroll, we'd get a lot of requests for like popular shows or shows that were kind of under the radar that we could reasonably try to go get.

341
00:30:54,240 --> 00:30:55,520
And we'd go try to get it.

342
00:30:55,680 --> 00:30:58,320
If we got it, we'd email them and say, hey, this is now on the service.

343
00:30:58,560 --> 00:30:59,280
Do you want to come back?

344
00:30:59,680 --> 00:31:03,440
and maybe potentially offer some kind of discount to come back or trial period.

345
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:05,440
It's kind of an intuition thing.

346
00:31:05,680 --> 00:31:14,640
I think most operators will probably be able to suss out, okay, this person just probably asked accidentally signed up or totally misunderstood our value prop before they signed up.

347
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:16,480
This person's pretty close.

348
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:22,800
And I think we could win them back if we just made some slight adjustments or slightly bent our product in this direction.

349
00:31:23,360 --> 00:31:36,160
It's fascinating too in the consumer space just how big the numbers can be, is that if you have product market fit adjacency where people are coming in for some reason, but you're just not quite solving their problem.

350
00:31:37,280 --> 00:31:49,040
If you figure that out and go from 10, 20 percent trial start rate to 25 percent, 30 percent trial start rate, it can just make such a dramatic difference in your business.

351
00:31:49,920 --> 00:31:52,800
And it's not like growth hacking your paywall.

352
00:31:53,280 --> 00:31:54,960
You have to actually deliver the value.

353
00:31:55,120 --> 00:31:59,280
You actually have to figure out what they need and solve those problems.

354
00:32:00,160 --> 00:32:02,320
It feels like there's just so many competing things there.

355
00:32:02,480 --> 00:32:09,840
Like how much effort you put into new features, how much you're testing your paywall, how much you're offering discounts.

356
00:32:10,080 --> 00:32:12,320
There's just so many things to be thinking about.

357
00:32:12,880 --> 00:32:17,680
I mean, the thing about writing the newsletter has been, this is all just like a balancing act.

358
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:20,320
And it just takes high judgment decision making.

359
00:32:20,560 --> 00:32:24,720
There's no universal right answer to balancing between all these pieces.

360
00:32:25,840 --> 00:32:34,400
I think it's like getting a strong sense of your business and like what is driving revenue growth and then being mindful when things start ticking in different directions.

361
00:32:35,200 --> 00:32:46,240
One thing that we talked a lot about in the streaming world, it's like this pendulum swinging between different business priorities and growth and profitability and all these counter forces.

362
00:32:46,640 --> 00:32:50,640
And you're always just trying to like fine tune and tweak things and choose the right path.

363
00:32:50,960 --> 00:32:53,760
But it's all a balancing act and there's no universal right answer.

364
00:32:53,920 --> 00:33:02,960
And that's why it's been fun to write about the frameworks because I'm just trying to write out the constructs of how we made certain really hard but important decisions.

365
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:09,120
And one of the things you keep coming back to in a lot of your posts is that it really does depend on stage.

366
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:17,920
Like if you're kind of early and pre-product market fit, maybe don't worry so much about retention, which is kind of like counter advice to what a lot of people would expect.

367
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:24,960
Like, oh, you need to solve the leaky bucket first through all these retention tactics and win back campaigns and all that.

368
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:29,040
But when you're early, probably better to keep focus on product.

369
00:33:29,520 --> 00:33:36,720
It's interesting how you end up in these frameworks talking about the context around those different kind of growth stages.

370
00:33:37,280 --> 00:33:49,280
When you're pre-product market fit, you should really just be focused on trying to really find the sweet spot of what you want to do and the product you want to build and an audience.

371
00:33:49,760 --> 00:33:54,480
You should not be really worried about what your cancel flow looks like or overthinking price.

372
00:33:55,120 --> 00:33:59,120
It's really about a relentless focus on finding product market fit.

373
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:04,320
And I know that's like another opaque phrase, but that's all that really matters early on.

374
00:34:04,720 --> 00:34:10,480
And so one of the risks I have with writing the newsletter is there's a bunch of, I think, early stage.

375
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:20,960
writers on Substack reading it and I really don't want them thinking about running paid ads out of the gate or thinking about how to better retain long-term subscribers.

376
00:34:21,600 --> 00:34:27,120
It's a total different set of priorities and it's about just finding that initial traction, which is extremely difficult.

377
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:27,680
Yeah.

378
00:34:28,400 --> 00:34:37,120
Interestingly, though, in one of your posts, you do kind of mention that when retention is super low, it might actually be time to just completely rethink things.

379
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:46,800
So, I mean, it's going to vary business to business and Netflix looks way different than the kind of like I was talking about the Median subscription app does have fairly low retention.

380
00:34:47,440 --> 00:34:56,800
But yeah, how do you decide, you know, is retention reasonable and there's a path to more value or is it just so low you need to like rethink things?

381
00:34:57,280 --> 00:34:57,360
Yeah.

382
00:34:57,520 --> 00:35:03,840
If you have like a bunch of people downloading your app using it once and never using it again, I think you don't have product market fit at that point.

383
00:35:04,080 --> 00:35:05,920
point, it doesn't even have to be that extreme.

384
00:35:06,080 --> 00:35:13,280
If they use it three times in the first week, but then never come back, that could work for some consumer products, but for most probably not.

385
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:18,800
So I think retention is a part of evaluating whether or not you have found traction.

386
00:35:19,680 --> 00:35:29,600
But I don't think it's worth thinking too hard about, don't beat yourself up about like some of the retention stuff that we've written about, especially those growth tactics.

387
00:35:29,760 --> 00:35:35,680
You know, it's like offering discounts in the cancel flow or trying to solve the problem in the cancel flow.

388
00:35:36,080 --> 00:35:42,320
Yeah, those different growthy tactics, which are really aimed at catching people on the fence about canceling before they actually cancel.

389
00:35:42,640 --> 00:35:48,480
I would put all that stuff out in the future after you have enough subscribers for that to be meaningful.

390
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:49,760
Yeah, and that's a good point too.

391
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:55,040
I mean, I hadn't thought about it quite this way, but it's like, early on your product should be what retains, not growth hacks.

392
00:35:55,120 --> 00:36:12,720
Like, if you're having to work so hard on subscriber life cycle marketing and doing all these winback campaigns and offering discounts, then that's actually probably a sign that you need to double down and figure out what's wrong with the product, that the product, the value you're actually delivering, is not what's retaining them.

393
00:36:13,040 --> 00:36:13,760
Yeah, exactly.

